WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.667
Connecting an MCP client.

2
00:00:02.967 --> 00:00:04.706
Last time we built the server.

3
00:00:04.706 --> 00:00:08.533
Now your own app becomes the client on the other side of the wire.

4
00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:14.367
Your application acts as the MCP client, and a session has a lifecycle.

5
00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:17.000
It connects, spawning or reaching a server.

6
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.233
It runs the initialize handshake.

7
00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:21.200
Then it lists the tools on offer.

8
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.067
It routes the agent's tool calls through call tool.

9
00:00:24.367 --> 00:00:26.300
And finally it closes the session.

10
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.467
Here is how it fits into your agent.

11
00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:31.433
At the top is your agent loop.

12
00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:38.533
MCP tool schemas map almost one to one onto LLM tool formats, so a thin adapter converts them.

13
00:00:38.833 --> 00:00:41.133
Beneath it are sessions, one per server.

14
00:00:41.433 --> 00:00:44.074
And the servers can be yours or anyone's.

15
00:00:44.074 --> 00:00:46.200
The hardcoded tools dict is gone.

16
00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:48.700
Here it is in Python.

17
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.833
We describe how to launch the server.

18
00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:54.867
We open a client session over stdio and run the handshake.

19
00:00:55.167 --> 00:00:58.400
Listing tools gives us everything to hand to the model.

20
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:01.700
And call tool runs one by name, with its arguments.

21
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:04.633
Now upgrade the reference loop.

22
00:01:04.933 --> 00:01:11.167
At startup it opens sessions to two servers: your mission database and a community filesystem server.

23
00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:17.067
It converts their tool schemas for the model and routes each call to the session that owns the tool.

24
00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:20.315
Adding a third integration is now a config line.

25
00:01:20.315 --> 00:01:24.000
You are server author, client author, and protocol literate.

26
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.467
One warning to finish.

27
00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:30.833
Third-party servers run their code on your box, so vet what you plug in.

28
00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:32.946
Let's recap.

29
00:01:32.946 --> 00:01:35.000
Your app is the MCP client.

30
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.600
The lifecycle is connect, initialize, list, call, close.

31
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.967
A thin adapter bridges the schemas.

32
00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:43.466
And vet any third-party server.

33
00:01:43.466 --> 00:01:46.233
Next up: A2A, the Agent2Agent protocol.
